Third path: the data held by MSKPATH3 leaves one end-of-packet-terminated GIF packet per lifting of the mask.

What it fixes: the runtime queued the GIF channel's transfers while path 3 was masked and, when the mask went
off, handed the GS the whole queue at once. This game masks path 3, sends its uploads (image + palette) through
the GIF channel in one block, then runs a VIF1 stream of the shape lift / mask / FLUSHA / DIRECT <draw> over and
over: on the console each lifting lets the third path start its next packet and the FLUSHA waits for it to end,
so every upload reaches the GS just before the draw that uses it. With the whole block delivered first, later
uploads overwrote earlier textures (3,405 of 5,608 textured vertices of the English menu were drawn after an
overlapping upload on the port; 0 of 5,599 on the console; docs/journal/021).
Now: a lifting releases ONE packet from the front of the queue (cut at the tag with EOP set by walking the GIF
tags: PACKED, REGLIST and IMAGE payloads; the rest of a transfer stays at the front), in order, drained into the
GS before the VIF1 stream goes on. The queue drains completely when the mask is off at FLUSHA, and at the end of
a VIF1 transfer (processVIF1Data). Data arriving with the mask off still goes straight through, and the FBRST
reset (which lifts the mask itself) still flushes everything. A packet that is truncated or has no closing tag
is released whole. Only ps2_vif1_interpreter.cpp changes (no header).
Verified: tests/runtime/path3_release_test.cpp (order p3:1 p2:A p3:2 p2:B p3:3 p2:C fails without the patch),
the English menu and the Japanese variant on the game (docs/journal/021).
Based on: the reference emulator's Gif_Unit.h / Vif_Codes.cpp (GPL-3.0) behaviour, no code taken.

